NotesString/You can open this notes window for any project by selecting View->Notes...

This example shows the Synchronous Serial interpreter decoding serial data from a 
PS2 keyboard. The PS2 standard uses synchronous data with the addition of a start
bit similar to RS232.

DATA was connected to Black wire of an extension cable.

CLOCK was connected to Yellow wire of an extension cable.

Keys A, B, C, D, E and F were pressed sequentially and the resulting data captured.

Cursors A-D are placed at scan codes for letters A-D.

Key release scan codes (for these particular keys) get F0h added to original scan code.

Please note that all of the example files are "read only" so that they remain intact
for future reference. You can change settings in these files for learning purposes, 
but the changes cannot be saved. You can however use the "Save As" option to 
save an editable copy of any example file.
/
